alex honor is a superstar in the world  of professional rock climbing he's best  known as the first person to climb the 3  000 foot cliff at el capitan in yosemite  national park without any ropes it was  documented in the academy award-winning  free solo film now he is taking  filmmaking to a whole new level check  this out with a new virtual reality  movie called the soloist vr it shows him  climbing some of the world's most iconic  mountains what makes this so remarkable  is the stunning and i mean stunning  virtual reality that gives us a 360  degree view of these crimes alex honol  joins us right now from las vegas good  morning alex how are you doing wow good  good good morning guys  thank you for joining us now  you have done this before but it was  just a documentary this vr is something  special so what made you want to turn  your climbs into a virtual reality  experience  mostly i think the vr experience is a  pretty impressive way to share i mean  you guys got to see it it's just so much  more immersive than  conventional filmmaking and so it's a  cool way to be able to show what i love  about climbing with with people that i  care about you know my family gets to  see this and be like whoa that's what  it's actually all about yeah listen i  have to say alex whoa that's what it's  all about is right i felt like i was on  your back at certain points a couple of  times i know i'm still standing on  ground somebody had to hold me up  because i thought i was going to fall  into the abyss stomach was uneasy knees  got away it wasn't easy it's amazing it  really is it really is i can't say  enough about it what are you hoping  people will get out of it maybe you want  the reaction that i'm talking about  because it was like nothing i've ever  seen or felt  yeah i think i think that's part of it  for sure what i'm hoping that people  take from it is a sense of the the joy  that i get from free souling because  typically when people ask me about  climbing without a rope they're asking  about the risk of death and the danger  and those kinds of things they rarely  ask about the beauty of it and the the  positive side of the experience and i  feel like with the vr filmmaking  experience you can look around you can  see the birds you can see the view you  can enjoy the mountains behind you and  so  you know you get to experience some of  the positive sides of sowing like the  reasons that i love going into the  mountains  yeah alex it's tony here i mean i think  people need to understand at home that  what's so special about this documentary  is when you put on the headset you are  in essence the cameraman so the person  watching you can watch you or just take  in the mountain take in the sunset look  anywhere there is amazing moments when  i'm standing in the headset in your  kitchen  and you're talking with your wife yeah  if i get bored with the conversation i  can like wander over and look in your  cabinets it's wild stuff but you  wouldn't get bored of conversation with  the wife yeah no no i'm not saying  anything about that  i felt like a voyer i was like an  invisible man it's really incredible  it's hard to explain to somebody who's  not in it and we got we got them here i  really do feel like it's a breakthrough  i wonder for you alex since you do so  much training before you climb and  thinking about the roots does vr hold  the potential to maybe be a replacement  for actually physically going out at  times and getting that climbing buzz or  experience  i don't think that vr will ever be a  substitute for the actual uh sort of  body awareness of the movement of  climbing because you have to practice  the movement to really be able to do it  but i do think the vr can definitely  prepare you for a place you know it's  like by looking around and appreciating  the mountains it can take some of the  the newness out of going to a new  setting i mean for me watching the vr  film you know i watched the dolomites  and i was only there briefly while we  were filming this and i can look around  and look at the mountains and when i go  back to the dolomites it's gonna feel  familiar to me because i've now spent so  much time watching it you know in vr i  don't know i mean there is  i don't know there's interesting  opportunities with vr for sure  alex there are so many things about you  that impress me the preparation like  tony talked about um but one thing that  i kept thinking about is the  fearlessness that you approach these  climbs with i have to ask are you afraid  of anything do you have any phobias  yeah  maybe not phobias at this point but no  i'm definitely afraid of things just  like anybody else but the things that  you're seeing in the vr film are all  things that i've practiced for things  that i've trained for for a very long  time  you know it's  it makes sense to not be too afraid of  them because that's that's what i've  spent 25 years getting ready for  you have a new baby in the house a new  baby girl has it changed your risk  tolerance  for with baby june  yeah so far it hasn't changed too much  except that i'm much sleepier but uh  okay yeah tony can identify with that i  can definitely but i'm not upside down  on a rock right sometimes you feel like  it but alex ronald thank you so much for  joining us the soloist vr is available  today on oculus tv please go check it  out yeah it's worth it
 

